  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(-3,4)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x+1).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $1$ unit to the right  and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-3,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-3+1,4)=(-2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,4)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,-(4))=(-2,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-2,-4+2)=(-2,-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-3,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x+1)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(-3+1)=4\\,\u27f9\\,f(-2)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=-2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(-2)+2 \\\\  & =-4+2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-2,-2)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$",
